trūlofa (= Sw. ''trolofva'' = Dan. ''trolove''), betroth, < ''trūa'', faith, + ''lofa'', praise: see ''true'', ''n.'', and ''love''<sup>2</sup>, ''v.'' The elements are only ult. related.] '''I.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' One truly loved or loving; one whose love is pledged to another; a sweetheart.
{{smaller block|<poem>“Where gat ye your dinner, my handsome young man?”
“I dined wi’ my ''true-love.''”</poem>
{{right|''Lord Randal'' (Child’s Ballads, II. 249).}}}}
'''2.''' A plant of Europe and temperate Asia, ''Paris quadrifolia'': so named because its four leaves are set together in the form of a heraldic true-love knot. Also ''herb-truelove.'' See ''herb-paris'' and ''Paris.''—'''3†.''' A condiment for sweetening the breath.
{{smaller block|<poem>Under his tonge a ''trewe-love'' he beer,
For therby wende he to ben gracious.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Miller’s Tale, l. 506.}}}}
'''4†.''' An ornament, probably shaped like a true-love knot. ''Fairholt.''
{{smaller block|<poem>My lady gan me sodenly beholde,
And with a ''trewe-love'', plited many-folde,
She smote me thrugh the harte as blive.</poem>
{{right|''Court of Love'', l. 1440.}}}}
{{smaller block|Out of his bozome drawne foorth a lappet of his napkin, edged with a blu lace, and marked with a ''truloove'', a hart, and a D. for Damian; for he was but a bachelar yet.
{{right|''R. Laneham'', Letter (1565), in J. Nichols’s Progresses, etc.,}}
{{right|[of Queen Elizabeth, I. 462.}}}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' Indicating genuine love; affectionate; sincere. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Wash him fresh again with ''true-love'' tears.
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. II., v. 1. 10.}}}}

'''True-love knot.''' {{smaller|See ''knot''<sup>1</sup>. Also ''true-lovers’ knot.''}}

'''trueness''' (trö′nes), ''n.'' [< ME. ''trewnesse'', ''treownesse''; < ''true'' + ''-ness''.] The character of being

readings.c2s1:
true; truth; faithfulness: sincerity; reality; genuineness; exactness; accuracy.
{{smaller block|<poem>Clariz iherde thes ille reuthe
Of ''trewnesse'' and of trewthe.</poem>
{{right|''King Horn'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 65.}}
In ''trueness'', and so methinks too.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Cynthia’s Revels, iv. 1.}}}}

'''truepenny''' (trö′pen″i), ''n.'' [< ''true'' + ''penny''.] An honest fellow. [Familiar.]
{{smaller block|Say’st thou so? art thou there, ''truepenny?''
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, i. 5. 150.}}
<poem>Go, go thy ways, old ''True-penny!'' thou hast but one fault:
Thou art even too valiant.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Loyal Subject, i. 3.}}}}

'''truer''' (trö′ėr), ''n.'' A truing-tool.

'''true-stitch''' (trö′stich), ''n.'' Through-stitch: applied to embroidery exactly alike on both sides of the foundation.
{{smaller block|<poem>Sister, i’ faith, you take too much tobacco;
It makes you black within, as you are without.
What, ''true-stitch'', sister! both your sides alike!
Be of a slighter work; for, of my word,
You shall be sold as dear, or rather dearer.</poem>
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Case is Altered, ii. 3.}}}}

'''true-table†''' (trö′tā″bl), ''n.'' A table for playing hazard.
{{smaller block|There is also a bowling-place, a tavern, and a ''true-table'' [var. ''trey-table'']. ''Evelyn'', Diary (1646), p. 193. (''Davies.'')}}

'''truff<sup>1</sup>†''' (truf), ''v. t.'' [Origin obscure.] To steal. [Scotch.]
{{smaller block|Be sure to ''truff'' his pocket-book.
{{right|''Ramsay'', Lucky Spence.}}}}

'''truff<sup>2</sup>†''', ''n.'' A transposed form of ''turf''.
{{smaller block|<poem>No holy ''truffe'' was left to hide the head
Of holiest men.</poem>}}

