'''Trullan''' (trul′an), ''a.'' [< ML. ''trullus'', ''trullum'', a dome-shaped building, a dome, < L. ''trulla'', a scoop, ladle: see ''trowel''.] Pertaining to the council ''in trullo''—that is, in the ''trullus'', or domed room in the imperial palace in Constantinople. {{smaller|This epithet is usually given to the Quinisext Council, 691 (though the sixth Ecumenical Council also met in the ''trullus''), considered as ecumenical in the Eastern Church, but not so acknowledged in the Western. It allowed the continuance in marriage of the priests, and passed a number of canons inconsistent with Roman authority and Western legislation and usages. See ''Constantinopolitan''.}}

'''trullization''' (trul-i-zā′shọn), ''n.'' [< F. ''trullisation'', < L. ''trullissatio(n-)'', < ''trullissare'', trowel, < ''trulla'', a trowel: see ''trowel''.] The laying on of layers of plaster with a trowel. ''Imp. Dict.''

'''truly''' (trö′li), ''adv.'' [Early mod. E. also ''truely''; < ME. ''truely'', ''treuly'', ''treuli'', ''trewely'', ''treowliche'', < AS. ''treówlīce'' (= D. ''trouwelijk'' = MLG. ''truwlike'' = OHG. ''getriuwelīcho'', MHG. ''getriuwelīche'', ''getriulīche'', G. ''getreulich'' = Sw. ''troligen''), truly, < ''treówe'', true: see ''true''.] '''1.''' In a true manner; in accordance with truth. {{smaller|(''a'') In accordance or agreement with fact.}}
{{smaller block|He whom thou now hast is not thy husband: in that saidst thou ''truly''.
{{right|John iv. 18.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') With truth; truthfully; rightly.}}
{{smaller block|The King is ''truly'' charg’d to bee the first beginner of these civil Warrs.
{{right|''Milton'', Eikonoklastes, x.}}}}
{{smaller|(''c'') Exactly; accurately; precisely; correctly; unerringly; unmistakably; justly.}}

readings.c3s3:
¶
{{smaller block|Ye ought to allow them that time that best serues your purpose and pleaseth your eare most, and ''truliest'' aunsweres the nature of the ortographie.
{{right|''Puttenham'', Arte of Eng. Poesie, p. 88.}}}}
(''d'') Naturally; with truth to nature.
{{smaller block|A pageant ''truly'' play’d. ''Shak.'', As you Like it, iii. 4. 55.}}
(''e'') Sincerely; faithfully; loyally; constantly; honestly.
{{smaller block|We have always ''truly'' served you.
{{right|''Shak.'', W. T., ii. 3. 147.}}}}
(''f'') Certainly; surely.
{{smaller block|<poem>Certes ouersome know it shal surely,
And then in hert gret dole shall haue ''truely!''</poem>
{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 2798.}}}}
(''g''†) Verily.
{{smaller block|Jhesu answeride, and seyde to him, ''Treuli, treuli,'' I seye to thee, no but a man schal be born agen, he may not see the kyngdom of God.
{{right|''Wyclif'', John iii. 3.}}}}
'''2.''' According to law; legitimately.
{{smaller block|Leontes [is] a jealous tyrant; his innocent babe ''truly'' begotten.
{{right|''Shak.'', W. T., iii. 2. 135.}}}}
'''3.''' In deed; in truth; in reality; in fact: often used emphatically, sometimes expletively.
{{smaller block|''Treuly'' that is a gret Myracle of God.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 48.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Truely'' Aristotle himselfe in his discourse of Poesie plainely determineth this question.
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Apol. for Poetrie (ed. Arber), p. 35.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Truly'', madam, I suspect the house to be no better than it should be.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Woman-Hater, iv. 2.}}}}

